    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A screened porch is a roofed porch or veranda enclosed with insect mesh. It is common in the US, and increasingly seen here as screened verandas and garden rooms. It offers a lovely balance between the comfort of indoors and the pleasure of being outside. Because the space is constantly exposed to pollen, humidity, wind and changing temperatures, though, it needs a regular maintenance routine to keep it sound and looking good.</p>
<p>Neglect leads to brittle screens, rotting timber and a build-up of dust and pollen that makes the porch unpleasant to use. Following a simple seasonal plan keeps your outdoor retreat a comfortable extension of your home for many years. Always read the care guidance for your screen mesh, flooring and finishes too, as products differ.</p>

<h2 id="spring">1. Spring cleaning</h2>
<p>Spring is the most important time for porch maintenance. After winter, the porch is likely to be covered in dust, cobwebs and, before long, a yellow film of pollen.</p>
<p>Start by clearing the space completely: move furniture and rugs out onto the lawn or drive. Use a vacuum cleaner with a brush attachment to lift loose debris from the screens, the floor and the ceiling corners. Then wash:</p>
<ul>
<li>Mix warm water with a little mild washing-up liquid. Avoid harsh chemicals and bleach, which can damage the protective coating on fibreglass mesh.</li>
<li>Wipe down the frames and the screens with a soft sponge or microfibre cloth.</li>
<li>If the screens are very dirty, rinse them with a garden hose on a gentle spray.</li>
</ul>
<p>While you are cleaning, check the spline, the rubber cord that holds the mesh in its frame. Temperature swings over winter can make it pop out of the groove; press it back in with a spline roller or the blunt edge of a flat screwdriver. Finally, wash outdoor rugs and let them dry completely in the sun before bringing them back, so they do not trap moisture against the porch floor.</p>

<h2 id="summer">2. Summer airflow</h2>
<p>In hot weather, how comfortable the porch feels depends largely on airflow and keeping insects out, so summer is the time for mechanical checks and pest prevention.</p>
<p>Start with the ceiling fans. Dust the blades thoroughly, as a build-up of dust can unbalance a fan, make it wobble and wear the motor. Most fans have a summer setting that pushes air downwards for a cooling breeze, but the direction varies by model, so check the fan's instructions. If the fan has a light, switch off the power before cleaning out any insects that have collected in the shade. Any electrical repairs to fans or porch lighting should be carried out by a qualified electrician.</p>
<p>Summer is also peak season for midges and mosquitoes. Look over the screens for even the tiniest pinholes; a hole the size of a pencil lead is enough to let small insects in. A small dab of clear nail varnish works as a temporary fix for tiny punctures in fibreglass mesh, and a patch kit deals with larger holes. Check the draught strip and sweep on the porch door as well: if you can see daylight round the edges when the door is shut, insects will find their way in. Replace worn sweeps or seals to keep the door closing snugly.</p>

<h2 id="autumn">3. Autumn debris</h2>
<p>As leaves start to fall, organic debris becomes the main problem. The screens keep leaves out of the seating area, but they tend to collect against the base of the outside walls.</p>
<p>Damp leaves piled against the bottom rail hold moisture against the frame and the floor, which on a timber porch encourages rot and fungal growth. Clear the outside edge with a leaf blower or stiff broom, ideally weekly while the leaves are falling.</p>
<p>Autumn is also the time to check the gutters. If the gutters above the porch are blocked, rainwater overflows and pours straight down the screens. That sheet of water carries dirt and minerals that can stain the mesh and speed up rusting of metal fixings. Make sure downpipes direct water well away from the porch base, so the ground around it does not wash away or soften. Use a stable ladder for gutter work, with someone to steady it, or hire a professional if the gutters are high or hard to reach.</p>

<h2 id="winter">4. Winter protection</h2>
<p>Winter care is about protecting the porch and its contents. In colder spells, snow and ice can put strain on both the mesh and the roof.</p>
<p>If you get heavy snow, do not let it pile up against the screens. The weight of a drift can stretch the mesh or push a whole panel out of its frame. Brush snow gently away from the outside with a soft broom.</p>
<p>Inside, cover or store the furniture. Many outdoor fabrics are weather-resistant rather than weatherproof, and repeated freezing and thawing can make synthetic rattan brittle and rust metal frames at the joints. If you cannot move furniture into a garage or shed, use good-quality breathable covers. If your porch is designed to take removable glass or storm panels, swapping them in for winter, following the manufacturer's instructions, helps keep wind-driven snow and rain off the interior finishes.</p>

<h2 id="screen-repair">5. Screen repair</h2>
<p>The screens are the most vulnerable part of any porch. Over time they can sag under wind pressure or become brittle from sun exposure, so it helps to know when to repair and when to replace.</p>
<p>Small tears can often be mended with a patch kit, which is a small piece of mesh with an adhesive backing. If a screen has a large split, or has turned shiny (a sign the protective coating has worn away), it is time to replace the panel.</p>
<p>When you replace a screen, think about the material:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fibreglass:</strong> the most common choice, as it is flexible and easy to fit.</li>
<li><strong>Pet-resistant mesh:</strong> a heavier, thicker polyester mesh that stands up much better to claws and knocks; worth considering if you have cats or dogs.</li>
<li><strong>Aluminium or stainless steel:</strong> often preferred near the coast for coping with salty air, though harder to fit than fibreglass.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="flooring">6. Flooring care</h2>
<p>The porch floor takes a lot of foot traffic as well as the weather. Whether it is pressure-treated softwood, cedar or composite, it needs its own attention.</p>
<p>For timber floors, do a water test once a year. Sprinkle a few drops of water on the boards: if they bead up, the finish is still protecting the wood; if they soak straight in, it is time to reseal or restain, following the product label. Keeping the finish up helps stop boards warping and splintering. For composite boards, look for mould or mildew in the gaps between boards, which can restrict drainage.</p>
<p>If you use outdoor rugs, lift them and clean underneath at least twice a year. Grit trapped under a rug acts like sandpaper on the floor finish as people walk over it, and moisture trapped underneath can leave marks or discolouration. Letting the floor breathe and stay dry helps the whole porch last longer.</p>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Keep a small repair kit in the porch: a spline roller, a spare length of spline, a mesh patch kit and a soft brush. Most screen problems are quick to fix if you catch them the day you notice them.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Can I use a pressure washer on my screened porch?</h3>
<p>Avoid pressure washers on the screens themselves, as the force can stretch the mesh or blow it out of the frame. A garden hose on a gentle spray and a mild cleaning solution are safer. On the floor and frames, use a low setting and follow the flooring or frame manufacturer's guidance.</p>
<h3>Can I leave outdoor cushions on the porch all winter?</h3>
<p>It is best not to. Even in a screened space, damp and temperature swings can lead to mould and mildew inside cushion foam. Store cushions somewhere dry indoors, or in a sealed storage box, over the colder months.</p>
<h3>What is the best way to remove green algae from porch frames?</h3>
<p>Algae tends to grow on the shadier, north-facing sides of a porch. Use a diluted mild cleaner or one made for exterior surfaces, test it on a hidden spot first and follow the label. Scrub gently with a soft brush and rinse thoroughly.</p>
<h3>How do I stop my screens rattling in the wind?</h3>
<p>Rattling usually means the spline has loosened or the mesh has stretched. You can try re-tensioning the screen by removing the spline and refitting it, but often the best fix is a slightly thicker spline that grips the groove more tightly.</p>
<h3>How can I stop birds flying into the screens?</h3>
<p>Birds may see reflections of the sky or trees and not realise the mesh is a barrier. Small hanging chimes or window decals designed to help birds see the barrier can help them steer clear of the porch.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A little care each season keeps a screened porch comfortable, free of insects and sound for years, and stops a small tear or a soft board from becoming a bigger repair. If you are still planning yours, read our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-screened-porch">how to build a screened porch</a>, and for the deck or railings around it see <a href="/blog/deck-railing-maintenance-by-season">deck railing maintenance by season</a> and <a href="/blog/composite-deck-maintenance-by-season">composite deck maintenance by season</a>. Explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service for styling your porch or veranda, and to plan your outdoor space with a designer, <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
